course: dictionary
lesson: 9885
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Tag can mean a small label, a chasing game, touching someone in that game, or putting a label on something. «Your suitcase disappears behind a rubber curtain at check-in with a paper tag on it.» Here a tag is a label, because it is tied to the bag and carries information.
explain: Other words in the sentence show you the meaning. «You're it!» tells you that a game of tag has started, because someone must now chase the others. «Please tag me in the photo» uses tag as a verb: to put a name on someone in a picture online. Ask yourself: what is happening here?
explain: A price tag is a label that shows how much something costs. «A small number on a foreign price tag does not mean it is cheap; you must think what it is in your own money.» So a tag gives information, but you must check what that information means before you decide.
explain: The game can have a different name in different places. In some countries the chasing game is called «kvach», and the one who tags shouts «kvach!» or «ty kvach!». In English, the player shouts «You're it!». Different words, same game.
fact: A tag is a small label tied or stuck on something, such as a «price tag» or a «name tag».
fact: Tag is also a running game where one player chases and touches the others.
fact: To tag someone means to touch them in the game, or to put a label on something.
fact: To tag along means to go with others, as in «Tag along with us to the park».
fact: Tag can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action), so check the sentence to see which one it is.
exercise: What is the paper tag on the suitcase at check-in? | A rubber curtain | A plane | A chasing game | *A label
exercise: In the playground game, what does the player who is «it» do? | Hides from everyone | *Chases and touches the others | Sings a song | Pays for the sweets
exercise: What does «tag along» mean? | To wait at home | To pay for a ticket | To read a label | *To go with others
test: Why must you think carefully about a foreign price tag? | It is always free | It is a rubber curtain | *Because the number may be in another money | Tags are never printed
test: In Britain, what must a dog wear when it is out in public? | A paper tag on a suitcase | A safety collar that snaps open | A barcode | *A collar tag with its owner's name and address
exam: Which sentence uses tag as a verb? | *Please tag me in the photo online. | A name tag hangs on my coat. | The price tag is very small. | A barcode is printed on the tag.
